We calculate the velocity and acceleration by taking derivatives: \begin{array}{rcl} \mathbf{v}(t) \amp =\amp \left\lt -\left(2t\right),-\left(3t^{2}\right)\right> \\ \mathbf{a}(t) \amp =\amp \left\lt -2,-\left(6t\right)\right> \end{array} and then plug in t=1 to obtain \begin{array}{rcl} \mathbf{v}(1) \amp =\amp \left\lt -2,-3\right> \\ \mathbf{a}(1) \amp =\amp \left\lt -2,-6\right> \end{array}
It remains to determine the direction of motion. Since the graph is x as a function of y, we know the direction is either "Bottom-to-Top" or "Top-to-Bottom." Since \frac{d y}{dt}=-\left(3t^{2}\right) is always negative, y is always decreasing and we determine that the direction is "Top-to-Bottom".

