static.solution:
[Step 1.] Calculate the integrand.
We write out the vectors and compute the integrand: \mathbf{c}(t)=\left( e^t , e^t ,t\right) \mathbf{F} \left(\mathbf{c}(t)\right)= \left\lt 3z y^{-1},4x,-y \right> = \left\lt 3t e^{-t},4 e^t ,- e^t \right> {\mathbf{c}}'(t)= \left\lt e^t , e^t ,1 \right> The integrand is the dot product: \mathbf{F} \left(\mathbf{c}(t)\right)\cdot {\mathbf{c}}'(t) = \left\lt 3t e^{-t},4 e^t ,- e^t \right>\cdot \left\lt e^t , e^t ,1 \right>=3t e^{-t}\cdot e^t +4 e^t \cdot e^t - e^t \cdot 1= 3t+4 e^{2t}- e^t [Step 2.] Evaluate the integral.
The vector line integral is: \int_{\mathcal{C}} \mathbf{F} \cdot \,d\mathbf{\,s} = \int_{-7}^{7} \mathbf{F} \left(\mathbf{c}(t)\right)\cdot {\mathbf{c}}'(t) \,dt= \int_{-7}^{7} \left(3t+4 e^{2t}- e^t \right) \,dt=0+\int_{-7}^{7} \left(4 e^{2t}- e^t \right) \,dt= 2 e^{2t}- e^t \bigg|_{-7}^{7}= \left(2 e^{14}-e^{7}\right)-\left(2 e^{-14}- e^{-7}\right)= 2\left( e^{14}- e^{-14}\right)-\left(e^{7}- e^{-7}\right)\approx 2.40411\times 10^{6}

statement.p:
Note that this isn't a very pleasant integral to evaluate by hand (though we could easily find a numerical estimate for it). It's easier to find \int_C
abla f\cdot d\vec r as the sum \int_{C_1}
abla f\cdot d\vec r + \int_{C_2}
abla f\cdot d\vec r, where C_1 is the line segment from (0,0) to (1,0) and C_2 is the line segment from (1,0) to (1,\pi/2). Calculate these integrals to find the change in f.

So that the change in f = \int_C
abla f\cdot\vec r = \int_{C_1}
abla f\cdot d\vec r + \int_{C_2}
abla f\cdot d\vec r =

static.solution:
(a) The parameterization is \vec r(t) = (x(t),y(t)) = \langle t, \pi\,t/2angle. Then \vec r\,'(t) = \langle 1, \pi/2angle, so that \int_C
abla f\cdot d\vec r = \int_0^1 \langle -10xe^{-x^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left(5yight), 25e^{-x^{2}}\cos\mathopen{}\left(5yight)angle\cdot \langle 1,\pi/2angle\,dt = \int_0^1 \left(25 rac{\pi }{2}e^{-t^{2}}\cos\mathopen{}\left( rac{5\pi t}{2}ight)-10te^{-t^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left( rac{5\pi t}{2}ight)ight)\,dt. As noted, it's simpler to integrate on the pair of line segments C_1 from (0,0) to (1,0) and C_2 from (1,0) to (1,\pi/2). Parameterizing these in a manner similar to our parameterization of C, on C_1 we have \vec r = \langle t, 0angle and on C_2 \vec r = \langle 1, \pi t/2angle. Then \int_{C_1}
abla f\cdot d\vec r = \int_0^1 0\,dt = 0, and \int_{C_2}
abla f\cdot d\vec r = \int_0^1  rac{\pi}{2}\,25\,e^{-1}\,\cos(5 rac{\pi}{2}\,t)\,dt = 5 e^{-1}, so that the change in f is (5 e^{-1}) + (0) = 5 e^{-1}.
(b) The other way to find the change in f between these two points is to first find f. To do this, observe that -10xe^{-x^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left(5yight)\,\vec i + 25e^{-x^{2}}\cos\mathopen{}\left(5yight)\,\vec j =  rac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(5e^{-x^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left(5yight) ight) \vec i +  rac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(5e^{-x^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left(5yight) ight) \vec j = 
abla \left(5e^{-x^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left(5yight)ight). So one possibility for f is f(x,y)=e^{x^2}\sin y. Thus, \mbox{Change in } f igg|_{(0,0)}^{(1,\pi/2)} = 5e^{-x^{2}}\sin\mathopen{}\left(5yight)  igg|_{(0,0)}^{(1,\pi/2)}= 5 e^{-1} - 0 = 5 e^{-1}.

statement.p:
Let C be the positively oriented square with vertices \left( 0, 0 \right), \left( 1, 0 \right), \left( 1, 1 \right), \left( 0, 1 \right). Use
Green's Theorem to evaluate the line integral \int_{C} 6 y^{2} x \, dx + 5 x^{2}
y \, dy.

Find the surface area of the part of the sphere 
x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 64 that lies above the cone 
z = \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}

Find the area of the surface obtained by rotating the curve
y = 5x^{3}
from x = 0 to x = 7 about the x-axis.

static.solution:
{\mathbf{T} }_u =\frac{\partial \mathrm{\Phi }}{\partial u}=\frac{\partial }{\partial u}\left( u^2- v^2,u+v,u-v\right)= \left\lt  2u,1,1 \right>{\mathbf{T}}_v =\frac{\partial \mathrm{\Phi }}{\partial v}=\frac{\partial }{\partial v}\left( u^2- v^2,u+v,u-v\right)= \left\lt  -2v,1,-1 \right>
The normal is the cross product of {\mathbf{T} }_u and {\mathbf{T} }_v. That is:
\mathbf{n} (u,v)={\mathbf{T} }_u \times {\mathbf{T} }_v = \left|\begin{array}{rrr}
\mathbf{i} \amp \mathbf{j} \amp \mathbf{k} \\2u\amp 1\amp 1\\-2v\amp 1\amp -1
\end{array}\right|=\left|\begin{array}{rr}
1\amp 1\\1\amp -1
\end{array}\right|\mathbf{i}  - \left|\begin{array}{rr}
2u\amp 1\\-2v\amp -1
\end{array}\right|\mathbf{j}  + \left|\begin{array}{cc}
2u\amp 1\\-2v\amp 1
\end{array}\right|\mathbf{k} =-2 \mathbf{i} -2(v-u) \mathbf{j} +2(u+v) \mathbf{k}
= 2 \left\lt -1,u-v,v+u\right>
We compute the normal \mathbf{n}  ( u,v ) at (5,3):
\mathbf{n} (5,3) = 2\left\lt  -1,5-3,3+5 \right> =2 \left\lt -1,2,8\right> \quad \mathbf{(1)}



We denote the rectangle \mathcal{D}=\left \lbrace(u,v):\,5\le u\le 5.9,\,3\le v\le 3.9\right \rbrace. 


Using the sample point corresponding to u=5, v=3 we obtain the following estimation for the area of S=\mathrm{\Phi}(\mathcal{D}):
\mathrm{Area}(S)\approx \|\mathbf{n} (5,3)\| \mathrm{Area}(\mathcal{D})=\|\mathbf{n} (5,3)\| \cdot 0.9 \cdot 0.9=0.81 \|\mathbf{n} (5,3)\| \quad \mathbf{(2)}
Using \mathbf{(1)} we get 
\|\mathbf{n} (5,3)\|=2\sqrt{(-1)^2+ (2)^2+ (8)^2}=2\sqrt{69}
Substituting in \mathbf{(2)} gives the following estimation:
\mathrm{Area} (S)\approx 0.81\cdot 2\cdot \sqrt{69}\approx 13.4567\text{.}

li.p:
If S_1 is a rectangle with area 1 and S_2
is a rectangle with area 2, then 
\displaystyle 2 \iint_{S_1} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} 
= \iint_{S_2} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A}

static.solution:
We denote the faces of the cube by:
\begin{array}{rclrclrcl} S_1 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, OABC \amp S_2 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, DGEF \amp S_3 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, ABGF \\ S_4 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, OCDE \amp S_5 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, BCDG \amp S_6 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, OAFE \end{array}
\bullet On S_1 {\mathrm{\Phi }}_1( x,y )=( x,y,0 ) and {\mathbf{n} }_1= \left\lt 0,0,-1 \right>. Thus, \mathbf{F} \left({\mathrm{\Phi }}_1( x,y )\right)\cdot {\mathbf{n} }_1= \left\lt 0,0, e^y \right>\cdot \left\lt 0,0,-1 \right> =- e^y \bullet On S_2 {\mathrm{\Phi }}_2( x,y )=( x,y,4 ) and {\mathbf{n} }_2= \left\lt 0,0,1 \right>. Thus, \mathbf{F} \left({\mathrm{\Phi }}_2( x,y )\right)\cdot {\mathbf{n} }_2= \left\lt 0,0, e^{y+4} \right>\cdot \left\lt 0,0,1 \right> = e^{y+4} \bullet On any other surface S_i, 3\le i\le 6, we have \mathbf{F} \left({\mathrm{\Phi}}_1(x,y)\right)\cdot {\mathbf{n} }_i =0, because the z-component of {\mathbf{n} }_i is 0 and the x, y components of \mathbf{F} equal 0. Thus, \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_{S_1} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S}+\iint_{S_2} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S}= \int_0^{4} \int_0^{4} - e^y \,dx \,dy+\int_0^{4} \int_0^{4} e^{y+4} \,dx \,dy=\int_0^{4} \int_0^{4} \left(e^{y+4}-e^y \right) \,dx\,dy= {4}\int_0^{4} \left(e^{y+4}-e^y\right) \,dy= 4\int_0^{4} e^y (e^{4}-1) \,dy=4 \left(e^{4}-1\right) e^y \bigg|_0^{4}= 4\left(e^{4}-1\right)\left(e^{4}-1\right)=4\left(e^{4}-1\right)^2

(a) We parametrize the upper-hemisphere by, \mathrm{\Phi }: x=5 \cos\theta \sin\phi , y=5 \sin\theta \sin\phi , z=5 \cos\phi with the parameter domain: \mathcal{D}=\left\lbrace( \theta ,\phi ): 0\le \theta \lt 2\pi , 0\le \phi \lt \frac{\pi }{2}\right\rbrace The outward pointing unit normal is : \mathbf{n} = {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} We compute the dot product \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} on the sphere. On the sphere r=5, hence, \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-r}\,{\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-5} {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} = e^{-5}, which is constant on the hemisphere, so the integral is just \big(e^{-5}\big) (\text{area of the hemisphere})=e^{-5}(2)(\pi 5^2)=50e^{-5}. Of course one can obtain the same result by integrating \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_{\mathcal{D}} \left(\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} \right) \,\rho^2 \sin(\phi) d\phi \, d\theta=\int_0^{2\pi } \int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-5}\, 25 \sin\phi \,d\phi \,d\theta= 50\pi e^{-5}\int_0^{\pi/2} \sin\phi \,d\phi =50\pi e^{-5}\left(- \cos\phi \bigg|_0^{\pi/2}\right) = 50\pi e^{-5} (b) We parametrize the part of the sphere inside the first octant of the sphere by, \mathrm{\Phi }: x=4\cos\theta \sin\phi , y=4\sin\theta \sin\phi , z=4\cos\phi with the parameter domain: \mathcal{D}=\left\lbrace( \theta ,\phi ): 0\le \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2} , 0\le \phi \lt \frac{\pi }{2}\right\rbrace Again the outward pointing unit normal is (as seen above): \mathbf{n} = {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} We compute the dot product \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} on the sphere. On the sphere r=4, hence, \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-r}\,{\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-4} {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} = e^{-4}.
Again this is constant on the sphere, so the integral is \big(e^{-4}\big)(\text{area of the surface}). One eighth of the sphere lies inside the first octant so the area of our surface is one eighth of the area of the sphere. Thus the integral is \big(e^{-4})(1/8)(4\pi 4^2)= \frac{16}{2}e^{-4}
Of course one can obtain the same result by integrating: \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_{\mathcal{D}} \left(\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} \right) \,\rho^2 \sin(\phi) d\phi \, d\theta=\int_0^{\pi/2} \int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-4}\,16 \sin\phi \,d\phi \,d\theta=\frac{16\pi}{2} e^{-4}\int_0^{\pi/2} \sin\phi \,d\phi = \frac{16\pi}{2} e^{-4}\left(- \cos\phi \bigg|_0^{\pi/2}\right)= \frac{16\pi}{2} e^{-4}

statement.p:
Calculate \iint_{\mathcal{S}} f(x,y,z)\,dS For
x^2+y^2=16 \text{,} \quad 0\le z\le 9 \text{;}\qquad f(x,y,z)=e^{-z}
\iint_{\mathcal{S}} f(x,y,z)\,dS=

static.solution:
z=g( x,y )= x^{1/3} and use the formula for the surface integral over a graph: \iint_S f( x,y,z ) \,dS=\iint_{\mathcal{D}} f\left(x,y,g( x,y )\right)\sqrt{1+ g_x^2+ g_y^2} \,dx \,dy\ \quad \mathbf{(1)} where \mathcal{D} is the square \left[0,{11}^{-\frac32}\right]\times \left[0,{11}^{-\frac32}\right] in the xy-plane. We compute the integrand in \mathbf{(1)}: g_x = \frac13 x^{-2/3},\quad g_y = 0 \Downarrow \sqrt{1+g_x^2+g_y^2}=\sqrt{1+\frac19 x^{-4/3}} f\left(x,y,g(x,y)\right) = x Substituting in \mathbf{(1)} we get: \iint_S f( x,y,z ) \,dS= \int_0^{{11}^{-\frac32}} \int_0^{{11}^{-\frac32}} x\sqrt{1+\frac19 x^{-4/3}} \,dx \,dy={11}^{-\frac32} \int_0^{{11}^{-\frac32}} x\sqrt{1+\frac19 x^{-4/3}} \,dx We compute the integral using the substitution \frac13 x^{-2/3}= \tan \theta. Then: \sqrt{1+\frac19 x^{-4/3}} = \sqrt{1+{\tan}^2 \theta }=\frac{1}{\cos \theta } -\frac29 x^{-5/3} \,dx = \frac{1}{\cos^2\theta } \,d\theta \Downarrow x \,dx=-\frac92\cdot \frac{1}{\cos^2\theta } x^{8/3} \,d\theta =-\frac{1}{18} \frac{\cos^2\theta }{\sin^4\theta }d\theta Hence: \sqrt{1+\frac19 x^{-4/3}}\cdot x \,dx=-\frac{1}{18} \frac{\cos \theta }{\sin^4\theta } \,d\theta We obtain the following integral, which we compute by substituting u= \sin \theta, \,du= \cos \theta \,d\theta :
With a=3\text{,} \quad b=11\text{,} \quad c=\sqrt{130} \quad \text{and } \alpha={\tan}^{-1} \left(\frac{11}3\right)
\iint_S f( x,y,z ) \,dS= {11}^{-\frac32} \int_{\pi/2}^{{\tan}^{-1} \left(\frac{11}3\right)} -\frac{1}{18} \frac{\cos \theta }{\sin^4\theta } \,d\theta=-\frac{{11}^{-\frac32}}{18}\int_1^{\sin \left({\tan}^{-1} \left(\frac{11}3\right)\right)=\frac{11}{\sqrt{130}}} \frac{1}{ u^4} \,du= \frac{{11}^{-\frac32}}{54}\cdot \frac{1}{u^3}\bigg|_1^{\frac{11}{\sqrt{130}}}= \frac{{11}^{-\frac32}}{54}\left(\frac{130\sqrt{130}}{1331}-1\right) \approx 5.76729\times 10^{-5}

statement.p:
Use the divergence theorem to calculate the flux of 
the vector field \vec{F}(x,y,z) = x^3 \vec{i}
+ y^3 \vec{j} + z^3 \vec{k}
out of the closed, outward-oriented surface S 
bounding the solid x^2 + y^2 \leq 16, \ 
0 \leq z \leq 4.

static.solution:
\mbox{div}\,{\vec{F}} = 8x + 4y + 8z. So, if W is the interior of the box, we have \int_{S}\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} = \int_{W}(8x + 4y + 8z)dV = \int_{3}^{5}\int_{0}^{1}\int_{0}^{1}(8x + 4y + 8z)dz\,dy\,dx. Integrating through each of the integrals in turn, we have \int_{S}\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} = \int_{3}^{5}\int_{0}^{1} 8x\,z + 4y\,z + 4z^{2}\bigg|_{z=0}^{z=1}\, dy\,dx = \int_{3}^{5}\int_{0}^{1} 8 x + 4 y + 4\,dy\,dx = \int_{3}^{5} 8 x y + 2 y^2 + 4 y\bigg|_{y=0}^{y=1}\,dx = \int_{3}^{5} 8 x + 2 + 4\, dx = 4 x^2 + 2 x + 4 x\bigg|_{x=3}^{x=5} = 76.
Finding flux directly, note that on the faces x=5 and x=3 the normal vectors to the surfaces are \vec i and -\vec i respectively, so that \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 4x^{2}\,dy\,dz (where x is evaluated at 5 or 3), and similarly on y = 1 and y = 0, and z = 1 and z = 0, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 2y^{2}\,dx\,dz and \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 4z^{2}\,dx\,dy, respectively. Thus on y = 0 and z = 0 the flux is zero. On the remaining faces, we have
(1) On x = 5, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{0}^{1}\int_{0}^{1}\,100\,dz\,dy = 100;
(2) On x = 3, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{0}^{1}\int_{0}^{1}\,36\,dz\,dy = -36;
(3) On y = 1, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{3}^{5}\int_{0}^{1}\,2\,dz\,dx = 4; and
(4) On z = 1, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{3}^{5}\int_{0}^{1}\,4\,dy\,dx = 8. Thus the total flux is the sum of these, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = 76.

